# Canonical identities for git log / shortlog / blame.
#
# The initial-import commit was produced on a work machine and picked up
# jim.galasyn@confluent.io from a global ~/.gitconfig. Because that commit had not
# been pushed anywhere when it landed here, it was AMENDED rather than mapped — no
# clone existed to break, so the work address is genuinely gone from this history
# rather than merely hidden. Verify: `git log --format='%ae' | grep -c confluent`
# should print 0 for the import.
#
# The mapping below is therefore NOT for that commit. It is for a second cause that
# no git config on any machine can prevent: when a PR is merged on GitHub, GitHub
# composes the merge commit itself and authors it with the account's PRIMARY email,
# which is the work address. This fires on merge commits as well as squash merges,
# and it is live. Expect one per merged PR. A local `git merge --no-ff` is authored
# from your own config and avoids it, where repo rulesets allow that path.
#
# No count is written here, because any number would be stale by the next merge.
# Recompute with:
#   git log --format='%an <%ae>' | sort | uniq -c
#
# Per-machine guard for the first cause (the repo cannot enforce it; a new machine is
# unguarded until set up — check `git config user.email` before the first commit):
#   - work machine: conditional include keyed on the remote URL, so the personal
#     identity attaches to any clone of this repo anywhere on disk —
#       [includeIf "hasconfig:remote.*.url:https://github.com/JimGalasyn/**"]
#               path = ~/.gitconfig-personal
#   - Bender: global user.email is the personal address; the work address is not
#     configured at all.
#
# The author name appears both as "Jim" and as "Jim Galasyn"; both map to one form.
#
# Format: Proper Name <proper@email> <commit@email>

Jim Galasyn <jim.galasyn@hotmail.com> <jim.galasyn@confluent.io>
Jim Galasyn <jim.galasyn@hotmail.com> <jim.galasyn@hotmail.com>
